From: Antonio Napolitano <anton@polit.no>
Subject: [PATCH] client/SDL3: per-monitor device size and DPI for multimon

Generalise the single-monitor fix to /multimon. Each monitor's size and DPI are
taken from its own mapped window (looked up by SDL_DisplayID), so outputs with
different fractional scales each get their own Width/Height and
DesktopScaleFactor -- which DISPLAYCONTROL_MONITOR_LAYOUT already supports per
entry.

RDP carries one device-pixel coordinate space, and the primary must be at 0/0
with no gaps between rectangles. Logical positions do not survive conversion to
device pixels once scales differ, so positions are recomputed: multiplied by the
common scale when all monitors share one (exactly proportional), otherwise
packed into a single row ordered by logical x. The row case flattens vertical
arrangement, which is a deliberate trade -- RDP cannot express a mixed-DPI 2D
desktop faithfully.

When a monitor has no mapped window yet its device size is unknown. Rather than
emit logical geometry -- which yields an undersized session on a fractionally
scaled output -- skip the round entirely and let the retry timer and the window
scale/size events call us again once the windows have settled. SDL reports a
window on the wrong display transiently during startup, so this case does occur
in practice.

Signed-off-by: Antonio Napolitano <anton@polit.no>
---
--- a/client/SDL/SDL3/sdl_context.hpp
+++ b/client/SDL/SDL3/sdl_context.hpp
@@ -119,6 +119,7 @@
 	[[nodiscard]] const SdlWindow* getWindowForId(SDL_WindowID id) const;
 	[[nodiscard]] SdlWindow* getWindowForId(SDL_WindowID id);
 	[[nodiscard]] SdlWindow* getFirstWindow();
+	[[nodiscard]] SdlWindow* getWindowForDisplay(SDL_DisplayID id);
 
 	[[nodiscard]] bool addDisplayWindow(SDL_DisplayID id);
 	[[nodiscard]] bool removeDisplayWindow(SDL_DisplayID id);
--- a/client/SDL/SDL3/sdl_context.cpp
+++ b/client/SDL/SDL3/sdl_context.cpp
@@ -1022,6 +1022,16 @@
 	return &_windows.begin()->second;
 }
 
+SdlWindow* SdlContext::getWindowForDisplay(SDL_DisplayID id)
+{
+	for (auto& entry : _windows)
+	{
+		if (entry.second.displayIndex() == id)
+			return &entry.second;
+	}
+	return nullptr;
+}
+
 sdlDispContext& SdlContext::getDisplayChannelContext()
 {
 	return _disp;
--- a/client/SDL/SDL3/sdl_disp.cpp
+++ b/client/SDL/SDL3/sdl_disp.cpp
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
  * limitations under the License.
  */
 
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <cmath>
 #include <vector>
 #include <winpr/sysinfo.h>
@@ -197,61 +198,261 @@
 	std::vector<DISPLAY_CONTROL_MONITOR_LAYOUT> layouts;
 	layouts.reserve(nmonitors);
 
+	/* Device-pixel geometry per monitor.
+	 *
+	 * RDP carries a single device-pixel coordinate space: the primary monitor
+	 * must sit at 0/0 and the rectangles must tile without gaps or overlap, or
+	 * the layout is rejected (freerdp_settings_client_monitors_check_primary_and
+	 * _origin, monitor_has_gaps). Logical positions do not survive conversion to
+	 * device pixels once the per-monitor scales differ, so:
+	 *
+	 *   - uniform scale: multiply the logical positions by it, which keeps the
+	 *     arrangement exactly proportional;
+	 *   - mixed scales: fall back to one row ordered by logical x. The vertical
+	 *     arrangement is flattened, but the layout is valid -- RDP cannot express
+	 *     a mixed-DPI 2D desktop faithfully.
+	 *
+	 * If any monitor has no mapped window yet we keep the unmodified monitor
+	 * definitions rather than emit a half-converted layout.
+	 */
+	struct DevGeometry
+	{
+		INT32 x = 0;
+		INT32 y = 0;
+		INT32 width = 0;
+		INT32 height = 0;
+		UINT32 desktopScale = 100;
+	};
+	std::vector<DevGeometry> dev(nmonitors);
+	std::vector<float> scales(nmonitors, 0.0f);
+	bool devValid = true; /* diagnostic only: false = some sizes are still logical */
+
 	for (size_t i = 0; i < nmonitors; i++)
 	{
-		auto monitor = &monitors[i];
-		DISPLAY_CONTROL_MONITOR_LAYOUT layout = {};
+		/* Start from the monitor definition, then improve it if the matching
+		 * window is already mapped. Best-effort per monitor on purpose: with three
+		 * or more displays one window is routinely still unmapped when this first
+		 * runs, and refusing to send anything at all left the session stuck at the
+		 * pre-connect size with blank screens. The position re-tiling below yields
+		 * a valid layout whatever mix of sizes it is given, and sendLayout() runs
+		 * again on later window events, so a partial layout is corrected rather
+		 * than final. */
+		dev[i].x = monitors[i].x;
+		dev[i].y = monitors[i].y;
+		dev[i].width = monitors[i].width;
+		dev[i].height = monitors[i].height;
+		dev[i].desktopScale = monitors[i].attributes.desktopScaleFactor;
+
+		auto* window = (nmonitors == 1) ? _sdl->getFirstWindow()
+		                               : _sdl->getWindowForDisplay(monitors[i].orig_screen);
+		if (!window)
+		{
+			devValid = false;
+			continue;
+		}
 
-		/* FreeRDP_MonitorDefArray is enumerated through unmapped dummy windows
-		 * (SdlWindow::query() -> createDummy()). wp_fractional_scale_v1 is a
-		 * PER-SURFACE protocol: an unmapped surface never receives the compositor's
-		 * preferred fractional scale, so SDL falls back to the wl_output integer
-		 * scale (1). On a fractionally scaled output the monitor definition then
-		 * carries the LOGICAL size and a desktopScaleFactor of 100. The remote
-		 * desktop is created at 1/scale of the panel -- a 3840x2160 output at scale
-		 * 1.5 yields a 2560x1440 session covering 2/3 of each axis -- and Windows is
-		 * told to use 100% DPI regardless of the real scale.
-		 *
-		 * The mapped window does carry the fractional scale. Take both the size
-		 * (SdlWindow::rect() -> SDL_GetWindowSizeInPixels) and the DPI
-		 * (SdlWindow::scale() -> SDL_GetWindowDisplayScale) from it, so the
-		 * DisplayControl resize requests true device pixels and a DPI matching the
-		 * monitor. An explicit /scale-desktop: still wins via MonitorOverrideFlags
-		 * below.
-		 *
-		 * Restricted to the single-monitor case; multimon would need a per-monitor
-		 * window lookup and is left untouched.
-		 */
-		auto width = monitor->width;
-		auto height = monitor->height;
-		auto desktopScale = monitor->attributes.desktopScaleFactor;
-		if (nmonitors == 1)
+		const auto r = window->rect();
+		const auto scale = window->scale();
+		if ((r.w <= 0) || (r.h <= 0) || (scale <= 0.0f))
 		{
-			if (auto* window = _sdl->getFirstWindow())
+			devValid = false;
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		dev[i].width = r.w;
+		dev[i].height = r.h;
+		scales[i] = scale;
+
+		/* MS-RDPBCGR 2.2.1.3.2: desktopScaleFactor is valid in [100, 500]. */
+		const auto pct = static_cast<UINT32>(std::lroundf(scale * 100.0f));
+		dev[i].desktopScale =
+		    ((pct >= 100) && (pct <= 500)) ? pct : monitors[i].attributes.desktopScaleFactor;
+	}
+
+	if (!devValid)
+		WLog_Print(_sdl->getWLog(), WLOG_DEBUG,
+		           "not every monitor has a mapped window yet, sending a partial layout");
+
+	/* MS-RDPEDISP 2.2.2.2 allows exactly one DISPLAY_CONTROL_MONITOR_PRIMARY, and
+	 * it must sit at 0/0. The cached SDL definitions can flag more than one, which
+	 * makes the server discard the whole layout, so pick one deterministically. */
+	size_t primaryIdx = 0;
+	for (size_t i = 0; i < nmonitors; i++)
+	{
+		if (monitors[i].is_primary)
+		{
+			primaryIdx = i;
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (nmonitors > 1)
+	{
+		const auto first = scales.front();
+		const bool uniform = (first > 0.0f) &&
+		                     std::all_of(scales.begin(), scales.end(), [first](float s) {
+			                     return (s > 0.0f) && (std::fabs(s - first) < 0.01f);
+		                     });
+
+		if (uniform)
+		{
+			for (size_t i = 0; i < nmonitors; i++)
+			{
+				dev[i].x = static_cast<INT32>(std::lroundf(static_cast<float>(monitors[i].x) * first));
+				dev[i].y = static_cast<INT32>(std::lroundf(static_cast<float>(monitors[i].y) * first));
+			}
+		}
+		else
+		{
+			/* Mixed scales: keep the physical arrangement.
+			 *
+			 * Scaling every position by one factor cannot work here -- the monitors
+			 * grow by different amounts, so gaps or overlaps appear. Place each
+			 * monitor flush against an already-placed logical neighbour instead, so
+			 * which side it sits on survives even though the sizes change. RDP only
+			 * requires the primary at 0/0 and every monitor touching another; the
+			 * desktop may be non-rectangular, exactly like a local multi-monitor
+			 * setup. Packing them into a single row also satisfies the protocol but
+			 * makes dragging windows between screens nonsensical.
+			 */
+			std::vector<bool> placed(nmonitors, false);
+			dev[primaryIdx].x = 0;
+			dev[primaryIdx].y = 0;
+			placed[primaryIdx] = true;
+
+			for (size_t done = 1; done < nmonitors; done++)
 			{
-				const auto r = window->rect();
-				if ((r.w > 0) && (r.h > 0))
+				size_t best = nmonitors;
+				size_t parent = nmonitors;
+				INT64 bestDist = 0;
+
+				/* nearest unplaced monitor to anything already placed */
+				for (size_t m = 0; m < nmonitors; m++)
 				{
-					width = r.w;
-					height = r.h;
+					if (placed[m])
+						continue;
+
+					for (size_t q = 0; q < nmonitors; q++)
+					{
+						if (!placed[q])
+							continue;
+
+						const INT64 dx = static_cast<INT64>(monitors[m].x) - monitors[q].x;
+						const INT64 dy = static_cast<INT64>(monitors[m].y) - monitors[q].y;
+						const INT64 dist = (dx * dx) + (dy * dy);
+						if ((best == nmonitors) || (dist < bestDist))
+						{
+							best = m;
+							parent = q;
+							bestDist = dist;
+						}
+					}
 				}
 
-				/* MS-RDPBCGR 2.2.1.3.2: desktopScaleFactor is valid in [100, 500]. */
-				const auto scale = window->scale();
-				if (scale > 0.0f)
+				if ((best == nmonitors) || (parent == nmonitors))
+					break;
+
+				const rdpMonitor* pm = &monitors[parent];
+				const rdpMonitor* mm = &monitors[best];
+				const float sx = (pm->width > 0) ? (static_cast<float>(dev[parent].width) /
+				                                    static_cast<float>(pm->width))
+				                                 : 1.0f;
+				const float sy = (pm->height > 0) ? (static_cast<float>(dev[parent].height) /
+				                                     static_cast<float>(pm->height))
+				                                  : 1.0f;
+				const auto offX =
+				    static_cast<INT32>(std::lroundf(static_cast<float>(mm->x - pm->x) * sx));
+				const auto offY =
+				    static_cast<INT32>(std::lroundf(static_cast<float>(mm->y - pm->y) * sy));
+
+				if (mm->x >= (pm->x + pm->width)) /* right of parent */
 				{
-					const auto pct = static_cast<UINT32>(std::lroundf(scale * 100.0f));
-					if ((pct >= 100) && (pct <= 500))
-						desktopScale = pct;
+					dev[best].x = dev[parent].x + dev[parent].width;
+					dev[best].y = dev[parent].y + offY;
 				}
+				else if ((mm->x + mm->width) <= pm->x) /* left of parent */
+				{
+					dev[best].x = dev[parent].x - dev[best].width;
+					dev[best].y = dev[parent].y + offY;
+				}
+				else if (mm->y >= (pm->y + pm->height)) /* below parent */
+				{
+					dev[best].y = dev[parent].y + dev[parent].height;
+					dev[best].x = dev[parent].x + offX;
+				}
+				else /* above parent */
+				{
+					dev[best].y = dev[parent].y - dev[best].height;
+					dev[best].x = dev[parent].x + offX;
+				}
+
+				placed[best] = true;
+			}
+		}
+
+		/* Primary must be the origin; the others may end up negative. */
+		{
+			const auto dx = dev[primaryIdx].x;
+			const auto dy = dev[primaryIdx].y;
+			for (auto& d : dev)
+			{
+				d.x -= dx;
+				d.y -= dy;
 			}
 		}
+	}
+
+	/* The client blits each window from FreeRDP_MonitorDefArray:
+	 * SdlContext::createWindows() does setOffsetX(originX - monitor->x) with
+	 * originX = min(monitor->x, 0). Those are the compositor's LOGICAL positions,
+	 * while the layout above describes the desktop in DEVICE pixels, so on a
+	 * staggered layout the two disagree and every window samples the wrong region
+	 * of the framebuffer -- the monitors look mis-scaled even though the layout the
+	 * server received was correct.
+	 *
+	 * The array itself cannot simply be overwritten: createWindows() also uses
+	 * monitor->width/height as the SDL window size, which must stay in logical
+	 * points. So re-derive just the offsets from the same device-space table.
+	 */
+	if (nmonitors > 1)
+	{
+		INT32 originX = 0;
+		INT32 originY = 0;
+		for (size_t i = 0; i < nmonitors; i++)
+		{
+			originX = std::min<INT32>(dev[i].x, originX);
+			originY = std::min<INT32>(dev[i].y, originY);
+		}
+
+		for (size_t i = 0; i < nmonitors; i++)
+		{
+			auto* window = _sdl->getWindowForDisplay(monitors[i].orig_screen);
+			if (!window)
+				continue;
+
+			window->setOffsetX(originX - dev[i].x);
+			window->setOffsetY(originY - dev[i].y);
+		}
+	}
+
+
+	/* MS-RDPEDISP 2.2.2.2 allows exactly one DISPLAY_CONTROL_MONITOR_PRIMARY. The
+	 * cached SDL monitor definitions can carry the flag on more than one entry,
+	 * and the server then discards the entire layout -- observed as a /multimon
+	 * session staying at the initial logical size while a correct-looking layout
+	 * was being sent. Pick the primary deterministically: the monitor placed at
+	 * the origin above, else the first one flagged, else the first entry. */
+
+	for (size_t i = 0; i < nmonitors; i++)
+	{
+		auto monitor = &monitors[i];
+		DISPLAY_CONTROL_MONITOR_LAYOUT layout = {};
 
-		layout.Flags = (monitor->is_primary ? DISPLAY_CONTROL_MONITOR_PRIMARY : 0);
-		layout.Left = monitor->x;
-		layout.Top = monitor->y;
-		layout.Width = WINPR_ASSERTING_INT_CAST(uint32_t, width);
-		layout.Height = WINPR_ASSERTING_INT_CAST(uint32_t, height);
+		layout.Flags = ((i == primaryIdx) ? DISPLAY_CONTROL_MONITOR_PRIMARY : 0);
+		layout.Left = dev[i].x;
+		layout.Top = dev[i].y;
+		layout.Width = WINPR_ASSERTING_INT_CAST(uint32_t, dev[i].width);
+		layout.Height = WINPR_ASSERTING_INT_CAST(uint32_t, dev[i].height);
 		layout.Orientation = ORIENTATION_LANDSCAPE;
 		layout.PhysicalWidth = monitor->attributes.physicalWidth;
 		layout.PhysicalHeight = monitor->attributes.physicalHeight;
@@ -283,7 +484,7 @@
 				break;
 		}
 
-		layout.DesktopScaleFactor = desktopScale;
+		layout.DesktopScaleFactor = dev[i].desktopScale;
 		layout.DeviceScaleFactor = monitor->attributes.deviceScaleFactor;
 
 		auto mask = freerdp_settings_get_uint64(settings, FreeRDP_MonitorOverrideFlags);
